WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.865
An eigenvector is a nonzero direction that a square matrix maps to a scalar multiple of

00:00:06.865 --> 00:00:13.530
itself. The direction’s line survives the transformation even when its length changes or its orientation reverses.

00:00:14.033 --> 00:00:19.937
This nonzero vector lies along the first coordinate axis. The diagonal map does not mix it

00:00:19.937 --> 00:00:21.413
with the second axis.

00:00:21.933 --> 00:00:26.553
The vector remains on the same line. The scalar multiplier is its eigenvalue.

00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:33.291
How will a negative eigenvalue behave after one, two and three applications? Pause here and make

00:00:33.291 --> 00:00:34.847
a prediction before continuing.

00:00:35.967 --> 00:00:43.548
Each application multiplies the previous coefficient by the same eigenvalue. Repeated action therefore produces a power.

00:00:43.548 --> 00:00:48.287
For our starting inputs, Eigenvalue: 2. After three maps: 8.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:57.280
Magnitude below one contracts; magnitude above one grows. A negative eigenvalue alternates the orientation on successive

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.460
applications. At zero the direction disappears.

00:01:00.967 --> 00:01:09.186
Eigenvalues describe signed action on special directions. Singular values instead describe nonnegative stretching and apply to

00:01:09.186 --> 00:01:10.727
rectangular maps too.

